cGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    lambda_pows: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    num_x: u32,\n    zc_out: &mut [Vec<EF>],\n    memory_limit_bytes: usize,\n) -> Result<(), KernelError> {\n    // Collect traces with constraints and their buffer sizes\n    let mut zc_traces_with_size: Vec<(&TraceCtx, usize)> = traces\n        .into_iter()\n        .filter(|t| t.has_constraints)\n        .map(|t| {\n            let buffer_size = pk.per_air[t.air_idx]\n                .other_data\n                .zerocheck_mle\n                .inner\n                .buffer_size;\n            let mem = zerocheck_batch_mle_intermediates_buffer_bytes(buffer_size, num_x, t.num_y);\n            (t, mem)\n        })\n        .collect();\n    if zc_traces_with_size.is_empty() {\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n\n    // Sort by buffer size descending for better bin packing (First Fit Decreasing)\n    zc_traces_with_size.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));\n\n    let num_x_usize = num_x as usize;\n    let mut batch_start = 0;\n\n    while batch_start < zc_traces_with_size.len() {\n        let (batch_count, batch_memory) = find_batch_end(\n            &zc_traces_with_size[batch_start..],\n            |(_, mem)| *mem,\n            memory_limit_bytes,\n        );\n        let batch: Vec<&TraceCtx> = zc_traces_with_size[batch_start..batch_start + batch_count]\n            .iter()\n            .map(|(t, _)| *t)\n            .collect();\n\n        if batch.len() == 1 {\n            // Single trace: use non-batch kernel\n            let t = batch[0];\n            if batch_memory > memory_limit_bytes {\n                tracing::warn!(\n                    air_idx = t.air_idx,\n                    intermediate_buffer_bytes = batch_memory,\n                    memory_limit_bytes,\n                    \"zerocheck: trace exceeds memory limit, using non-batch kernel\"\n                );\n            }\n            let rules = &pk.per_air[t.air_idx].other_data.zerocheck_mle;\n            let out = evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu(\n                t.eq_xi_ptr,\n                t.sels_ptr,\n                t.prep_ptr,\n                &t.main_ptrs_dev,\n                t.public_ptr,\n                lambda_pows,\n                rules,\n                t.num_y,\n                num_x,\n            )?;\n            let out_host = out.to_host()?;\n            zc_out[t.trace_idx].copy_from_slice(&out_host);\n        } else {\n            // Normal batch using ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder\n            tracing::debug!(\n                batch_size = batch.len(),\n                batch_memory,\n                memory_limit_bytes,\n                \"zerocheck: batching traces\"\n            );\n            let builder = ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new(batch.iter().copied(), pk, num_x)?;\n            let out = builder.evaluate(lambda_pows, num_x)?;\n            let host = out.to_host()?;\n\n            for (i, trace_idx) in builder.trace_indices().enumerate() {\n                let evals = &host[(i * num_x_usize)..((i + 1) * num_x_usize)];\n                zc_out[trace_idx].copy_from_slice(evals);\n            }\n        }\n        batch_start += batch_count;\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\npub(crate) fn evaluate_logup_batched<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    traces: &[TraceCtx],\n    pk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    d_challenges_ptr: *const EF,\n    num_x: u32,\n    monomial_num_y_threshold: u32,\n    logup_combinations: &[Option<LogupCombinations>],\n    logup_out: &mut [[Vec<EF>; 2]],\n    logup_tilde_evals: &mut [[EF; 2]],\n    memory_limit_bytes: usize,\n) -> Result<(), KernelError> {\n    let (low_traces, high_traces): (Vec<&TraceCtx>, Vec<&TraceCtx>) = traces\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|t| t.has_interactions)\n        .partition(|t| t.num_y <= monomial_num_y_threshold);\n\n    if !low_traces.is_empty() {\n        let logup_combs: Vec<_> = low_traces\n            .iter()\n            .map(|t| logup_combinations[t.trace_idx].as_ref().unwrap())\n            .collect();\n        let batch = LogupMonomialBatch::new(low_traces.iter().copied(), pk, &logup_combs)?;\n        let out = batch.evaluate(num_x)?;\n        let host = out.to_host()?;\n        let num_x_usize = num_x as usize;\n        for (i, trace_idx) in batch.trace_indices().enumerate() {\n            let fracs = &host[(i * num_x_usize)..((i + 1) * num_x_usize)];\n            let norm = low_traces[i].norm_factor;\n            if num_x == 1 {\n                logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx][0] = fracs[0].p * norm;\n                logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx][1] = fracs[0].q;\n            } else {\n                for (j, frac) in fracs.iter().enumerate() {\n                    logup_out[trace_idx][0][j] = frac.p * norm;\n                    logup_out[trace_idx][1][j] = frac.q;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    if high_traces.is_empty() {\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n\n    // Collect high traces with interactions and their buffer sizes\n    let mut logup_traces_with_size: Vec<(&TraceCtx, usize)> = high_traces\n        .iter()\n        .copied()\n        .map(|t| {\n            let buffer_size = pk.per_air[t.air_idx]\n                .other_data\n                .interaction_rules\n                .inner\n                .buffer_size;\n            let mem = logup_batch_mle_intermediates_buffer_bytes(buffer_size, num_x, t.num_y);\n            (t, mem)\n        })\n        .collect();\n\n    // Sort by buffer size descending for better bin packing (First Fit Decreasing)\n    logup_traces_with_size.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));\n\n    let num_x_usize = num_x as usize;\n    let mut batch_start = 0;\n\n    while batch_start < logup_traces_with_size.len() {\n        let (batch_count, batch_memory) = find_batch_end(\n            &logup_traces_with_size[batch_start..],\n            |(_, mem)| *mem,\n            memory_limit_bytes,\n        );\n        let batch: Vec<&TraceCtx> = logup_traces_with_size[batch_start..batch_start + batch_count]\n            .iter()\n            .map(|(t, _)| *t)\n            .collect();\n\n        if batch.len() == 1 && batch_memory > memory_limit_bytes {\n            // Single oversized trace: use non-batch kernel\n            let t = batch[0];\n            tracing::warn!(\n                air_idx = t.air_idx,\n                intermediate_buffer_bytes = batch_memory,\n                memory_limit_bytes,\n                \"logup: trace exceeds memory limit, using non-batch kernel\"\n            );\n            evaluate_single_logup(\n                t,\n                pk,\n                d_challenges_ptr,\n                num_x,\n                &mut logup_out[t.trace_idx],\n                &mut logup_tilde_evals[t.trace_idx],\n            )?;\n        } else {\n            // Normal batch using LogupMleBatchBuilder\n            tracing::debug!(\n                batch_size = batch.len(),\n                batch_memory,\n                memory_limit_bytes,\n                \"logup: batching traces\"\n            );\n            let builder =\n                LogupMleBatchBuilder::new(batch.iter().copied(), pk, d_challenges_ptr, num_x)?;\n            let out = builder.evaluate(num_x)?;\n            let host = out.to_host()?;\n\n            for (i, (trace_idx, norm_factor)) in builder.trace_info().enumerate() {\n                let fracs = &host[(i * num_x_usize)..((i + 1) * num_x_usize)];\n                if num_x == 1 {\n                    logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx][0] = fracs[0].p * norm_factor;\n                    logup_tilde_evals[trace_idx][1] = fracs[0].q;\n                } else {\n                    let numer: Vec<EF> = fracs.iter().map(|f| f.p * norm_factor).collect();\n                    let denom: Vec<EF> = fracs.iter().map(|f| f.q).collect();\n                    logup_out[trace_idx] = [numer, denom];\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        batch_start += batch_count;\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Evaluate logup for a single trace using non-batch kernel.\nfn evaluate_single_logup<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    t: &TraceCtx,\n    pk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    d_challenges_ptr: *const EF,\n    num_x: u32,\n    logup_out: &mut [Vec<EF>; 2],\n    logup_tilde_eval: &mut [EF; 2],\n) -> Result<(), KernelError> {\n    let air_pk = &pk.per_air[t.air_idx];\n    let out = evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu(\n        t.eq_xi_ptr,\n        t.sels_ptr,\n        t.prep_ptr,\n        &t.main_ptrs_dev,\n        t.public_ptr,\n        d_challenges_ptr,\n        t.eq_3bs_ptr,\n        &air_pk.other_data.interaction_rules,\n        t.num_y,\n        num_x,\n    )?;\n    let fracs = out.to_host()?;\n\n    if num_x == 1 {\n        logup_tilde_eval[0] = fracs[0].p * t.norm_factor;\n        logup_tilde_eval[1] = fracs[0].q;\n        // logup_out not set, will be handled directly from tilde eval in compute_batch_s\n    } else {\n        let numer: Vec<EF> = fracs.iter().map(|f| f.p * t.norm_factor).collect();\n        let denom: Vec<EF> = fracs.iter().map(|f| f.q).collect();\n        *logup_out = [numer, denom];","numLines":260,"startLine":400,"totalLines":736}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014bRwDFRcrTLyCaya4XEsMF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the MLE round sync pattern clearly. Let me also look at the `record_and_wait` function to understand the pipeline drain.\n\nThe `to_host()` implementation:\n1. `cudaMemcpyAsync` - non-blocking on the CPU side, queued on the stream\n2. `COPY_EVENT.lock().unwrap().record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)` - BLOCKS the CPU until ALL pending work on the stream completes\n\nSo every `to_host()` call drains the ENTIRE GPU pipeline for the current stream. This is exactly the same pattern that caused the stacked reduction bottleneck.\n\n\nLooking at `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`, I'm seeing multiple pipeline drains scattered throughout: the late logup and late zerocheck both call `to_host()`, and in the early logup section there are additional drains happening for the low traces monomial batch and then again for each high traces sub-batch.\n\nThe early zerocheck DAG also introduces drains per sub-batch, and then there's the high mono and low mono evaluations at the end. The actual number of pipeline drains per round really depends on how many sub-batches get created during the early evaluation paths—with 623 AIRs of varying sizes under APC 300, the sub-batching logic could be generating quite a few of them.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the late versus early distinction here. It's based on whether `round > n_lift`, and with a lift of 20—\n\n30 rounds and different `n_lift` values per trace, the split between late and early evaluations shifts each round. Early rounds have mostly \"early\" traces with high `num_y`, while later rounds see more \"late\" traces where `num_y` drops to 1.\n\nThe optimization I'm thinking about is batching all the kernel launches within a single round before syncing. Instead of launching a kernel, copying results, processing, then repeating, I could queue all the kernels first, do one sync, then copy everything at once. Since all evaluations in a round write to independent output arrays, they can all launch together before any host transfer happens.\n\nThe main obstacle is that the current structure immediately calls `to_host()` after each `evaluate()` call, which blocks the pipeline. I'd need to separate the launch phase from the readback phase—collect all the `DeviceBuffer` results, sync once, then copy them all without triggering additional syncs. The `to_host()` method uses `record_and_wait` which drains the pipeline, so I'd need an async copy variant that doesn't sync, followed by a single explicit sync point.\n\nThis mirrors the stacked reduction optimization that worked well. I'm leaning toward Option A: creating a `to_host_no_sync()` method that queues the copy without syncing, then calling `current_stream_sync()` once after all copies are queued. Let me verify if there's already infrastructure for this pattern.\n\nLooking back at how stacked reduction solved this—it consolidated to a single `to_host()` per round instead of per-window. For MLE rounds, I could do something similar: collect all the DeviceBuffers from evaluations and sync once before copying them back. The challenge is that each evaluation returns differently-sized buffers, so they can't merge into one. I'd need to keep them alive, sync the stream once, then copy each asynchronously.\n\nThe real issue is that `to_host()` calls `record_and_wait()`, forcing synchronization immediately. If I could skip that and queue all the copies first, then sync once at the end, I'd get the same batching benefit. So I'm thinking of creating an async variant that queues the copy without syncing, then calling a single stream sync after all copies are queued to ensure the data is ready.\n\nImplementing the async variant...\n\nThen I'd sync once after queuing everything, which populates all the host vectors at once. The main concern is whether the host vector needs to be pinned for this to work correctly.\n\nActually, looking at the CUDA docs more carefully, `cudaMemcpyAsync` from device to non-pinned host memory is synchronous anyway—each copy blocks until it completes. So using `record_and_wait` adds overhead on top of that synchronous behavior, making the async approach pointless for pageable memory.\n\nBut wait, let me reconsider the stacked reduction fix. The real win there wasn't about deferring syncs—it was eliminating the copies entirely by doing GPU-side atomic accumulation instead of copying per-window results and summing on the CPU. That reduced the number of transfers from many per round to just one final sum.\n\nFor logup_zerocheck, we can't apply that same optimization since we need the individual evaluation results, not aggregated sums, so we're stuck with per-evaluation copies regardless.\n\nThat said, I can still look at reducing pipeline drains. Even if `cudaMemcpyAsync` with pageable memory ends up being synchronous for the actual data transfer, the `record_and_wait` overhead is separate. Let me trace through what that function actually does.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5063,"cache_read_input_tokens":94217,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5063},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf8cf1a6-3d58-4ca0-8a0c-442b3fb4c97c","uuid":"6c9c4444-227f-4ae7-ad8e-6c09d3639fcd"}
