fers\n\nThe main benefit is concurrent kernel execution. With 4 OS threads, 4 streams, and small kernels, the GPU can process 4 AIRs simultaneously.\n\nBut there's a memory visibility issue: when I launch kernels from different threads using `cudaStreamPerThread`, those kernels need to read trace matrices that were uploaded on the main thread's stream. The per-thread default stream doesn't synchronize with other per-thread default streams, so I need to verify the memory is actually visible across threads.\n\nThe solution is to call `cudaDeviceSynchronize()` before spawning worker threads — this ensures all prior GPU work (including trace matrix uploads) is complete and visible to all subsequent streams. Then I can spawn worker threads that each launch kernels on their own per-thread stream, and those kernels will safely see the trace matrices.\n\nThe implementation groups AIRs across threads, each thread processes its group using the existing per-AIR evaluation code, and results are collected and written to the shared output buffer at non-overlapping positions.\n\nNow I'm examining the Round 0 loop to understand how the per-AIR processing works and how to safely parallelize the output writes. Each AIR writes to a different index in the batch output vector, so as long as threads write to distinct indices, there shouldn't be synchronization issues.\n\nThe challenge is that Rust's borrow checker won't allow multiple mutable references to the same Vec, even if they're accessing different elements. The cleanest approach would be to have each thread accumulate its results into a separate buffer, then merge everything after all threads complete.\n\nI'm settling on a multi-stream Round 0 strategy using OS-level threading, which should reduce GPU kernel time from around 520ms to 250-350ms through concurrent execution, while also hiding much of the CPU overhead that currently takes 143ms.\n\nNow I'm double-checking my performance estimates against the actual kernel distribution to make sure they're realistic. Looking at the performance breakdown across the four concurrent streams, I'm estimating a 2-3x speedup on GPU time which would bring the total down to 175-260ms, and with CPU overhead partially hidden, that's roughly a 40-52% improvement for Round 0. But I need to be more conservative—accounting for shared memory bandwidth contention, L2 cache conflicts, and scheduling overhead, a 1.5x speedup on GPU time is probably more realistic, pushing the time to around 350ms.\n\nEven with this conservative estimate, Round 0 improves from 663ms to 430ms (35% gain), and the overall STARK time drops by about 10%. At APC 0 where we have 99 large AIRs already saturating the GPU, concurrent streams won't help much—maybe 1.0-1.1x speedup at best—but that means no regression either, which is important.\n\nThis looks solid, so I'm moving forward with the implementation plan.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether a simpler work-stealing approach with a thread pool and atomic counter would be cleaner than managing CUDA streams. Each thread could atomically grab the next AIR index from a shared counter and process it, storing results in its slot—this naturally load-balances without complex grouping logic. The real challenge isn't thread management though; it's making the existing code thread-safe since Round 0 reads from shared structures like `pk`, `eq_xis`, and `sels_per_trace_base` that are currently accessed without synchronization.\n\nThe proving key itself likely isn't `Sync` because it contains `DeviceBuffer` objects with raw pointers, so I'd need to either wrap it in `Arc` or use `unsafe` to share it across threads. Rather than fighting this, maybe I should extract the read-only data needed per AIR and restructure the code to work with rayon's parallel iterators instead of managing OS threads directly.\n\nI could create work items that bundle together all the GPU-resident data each AIR needs—trace pointers, proving key rules, selector data, eq_xi trees—and process them in parallel since they're all read-only during Round 0. The GPU phase itself could also use per-thread CUDA streams to parallelize the work. Let me just commit to writing out the multi-stream Round 0 plan and sort out the implementation details as I go.\n\nActually, I should double-check the scope constraints first. Multi-stream Round 0 stays within stark-backend, only touches the prover's Round 0, and doesn't alter the protocol or verifier, so it's in bounds. The plan needs to be prototype-ready though, which means I have to be concrete about which files and functions change, what new data structures I need, how I'll manage threads and streams, and how memory synchronization works. Let me write that out now.\n\nWait, before I commit to this approach, let me think about whether there's a simpler optimization that could still move the needle.\n\nLooking at `fold_mle_evals`, it gets called once per MLE round. And `precompute_logup_combinations` has 791 instances per variant—if those are launched with per-launch overhead, batching them could help. But actually, checking the code more carefully, those precomputations happen before the MLE rounds start, not inside the loop, so that's not the bottleneck. I need to stop overthinking this and pick a direction.\n\nWeighing the three options: multi-stream Round 0 has the highest impact (150-350ms, 7-15%) and addresses the root cause of per-AIR overhead with many small AIRs, even though it's complex. MLE sync optimization is lower impact but higher confidence. Zero hash caching is low complexity but modest gains. I'm going with multi-stream Round 0 because the expected impact justifies the complexity, and it's a proven CUDA optimization pattern.\n\nBefore committing, let me verify the core assumption—that small kernels underutilize the GPU and concurrent execution would help. The RTX 4090 has 128 SMs, so I need to check if the nsight data supports this.\n\nFor small AIRs with height=8, the constraint kernel might only use 1 thread block across 1 SM, which is just 0.8% GPU utilization. Even running 4 concurrently gets us to only 3.1%. But here's the key insight: with 128 SMs available and 623 small AIRs in the workload, we could theoretically run 128 kernels concurrently instead of sequentially, which would give us far more than a 4x speedup.\n\nThe multi-stream approach has real constraints though. CUDA can only dispatch 32-128 concurrent kernels depending on hardware, and there's overhead from managing multiple streams and their command queues. With 4 streams, we'd distribute roughly 156 kernels per stream, executing sequentially within each stream but concurrently across streams. For the medium-sized kernels in the 100-500μs range, running 4 concurrently would use 16-128 SMs, which is much better utilization than the small kernels. I think this approach is worth implementing, though I'm starting to worry about the data copy overhead.\n\nThe COPY_EVENT mutex in `to_host()` would serialize all D2H transfers across threads, which would block concurrent execution if each thread calls it after every kernel pair. I could either collect GPU results into buffers and do all copies sequentially from the main thread afterward, or implement per-thread copies without the global mutex. The first option is cleaner but risks memory pressure with 1246 DeviceBuffers alive simultaneously, though they're small enough that it might actually be manageable.\n\nLooking at the actual buffer sizes in the current code, the output buffers are quite small—just a few kilobytes total across all 623 AIRs—so keeping them alive until the main thread does the batch D2H transfer is feasible and avoids the mutex bottleneck.\n\nThe real issue is that `cudaMallocAsync` allocations are tied to the stream they're created on, so if the worker thread's per-thread stream gets destroyed when the thread exits, the buffers might be freed prematurely. I could work around this by using regular `cudaMalloc` for the output buffers, keeping the worker threads alive until after D2H, or creating explicit streams on the main thread that outlive the workers.\n\nThe kernel wrappers are hardcoded to use `cudaStreamPerThread`, which limits flexibility. Another option is to use `std::thread::scope` to keep threads alive through the entire operation, then have each thread call `to_host()` directly within the scope before returning—this way the DeviceBuffers stay valid and the D2H transfers happen while threads are still running, with the COPY_EVENT mutex serializing the transfers.\n\nThe key insight is that kernel launches aren't serialized across threads since each uses its own stream, so multiple kernels can execute concurrently on the GPU while the to_host() calls are serialized by the mutex. to_host() calls are serialized by the mutex, but each to_host() is fast (because the kernel on that stream is already done, or finishes quickly.\n\nThe key concurrency benefit: while thread 1 is doing to_host() + process, threads 2-4 can launch their next kernels. The GPU keeps getting new work even while one thread is doing D2H.\n\nThis is actually similar to a 4-stage pipeline:\n- Stage 1: CPU prep\n- Stage 2: GPU kernel\n- Stage 3: D2H copy\n- Stage 4: CPU post-process\n\nWith 4 threads, stages 2-4 overlap across AIRs. The total time is bounded by:\nmax(total_cpu_prep, total_gpu_time, total_D2H, total_cpu_postprocess)\n\nWhere:\n- total_cpu_prep = sum(prep_i) but overlapped across threads\n- total_gpu_time = depends on concurrent execution\n- total_D2H = sum(D2H_i) serialized by mutex\n- total_cpu_postprocess = sum(postprocess_i)\n\nFor APC 300, I'm working through the numbers: CPU prep across 4 threads drops from 62ms to around 16ms, GPU time with concurrent streams improves to 130-200ms instead of 520ms, D2H stays at 62ms but serialized, and post-processing is about 20ms. That gives a total of roughly 130-200ms per round. But wait, that feels too optimistic—the pipeline isn't perfectly overlapped since each thread has dependencies between stages. AIRs sequentially. The to_host() in each thread blocks until the kernel on that stream finishes (because cudaMemcpyAsync with pageable memory is synchronous, waiting for preceding stream operations).\n\nSo within a single thread, the pipeline is still sequential per AIR:\nprep → kernel → D2H → postprocess\n\nBut across 4 threads on 4 streams, 4 AIRs are in different pipeline stages simultaneously.\n\nThe throughput improvement comes from:\n1. GPU concurrent execution: 4 small kernels run at the same time\n2. CPU/GPU overlap: while one thread does D2H+postprocess, other threads' kernels are running\n\nThe COPY_EVENT mutex adds serialization to D2H, but since D2H is fast (the kernel is already done on that stream), the mutex overhead is the event record+sync time, not kernel wait time.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the actual timing for each stage: CPU prep takes about 100μs, the constraint kernel runs for roughly 400μs on average, then D2H plus postprocessing takes 30μs, followed by the interaction kernel at 500μs average, and another 30μs for the final D2H and postprocess. That's roughly 1060μs per AIR total. With a single thread processing 623 AIRs, I'd expect around 660ms, which matches the observed 663ms. When I scale to 4 threads with 4 streams, the kernels become the limiting factor—if they run with perfect 4-way concurrency, the combined kernel time drops to about 225μs per AIR, bringing the effective per-AIR time down to around 385μs, though I need to reconsider how the 4x GPU throughput actually translates to the overall speedup.\n\nLooking at the kernel size distribution, I can see that larger kernels (over 1ms) won't benefit from concurrency since they already saturate the GPU, while smaller kernels in the 100-500μs range could see 2-3x speedup, and the tiniest ones under 100μs might achieve 3-4x improvement.\n\nWith 4 concurrent streams, the GPU time drops from 543ms to around 334ms with optimistic assumptions, or 418ms being more conservative at 1.3x average speedup. Either way, this translates to roughly 28-41% improvement on the full Round 0 time, saving somewhere between 185-269ms and bringing the total STARK execution time down to around 2087ms.\n\nBefore committing to this approach, I need to verify the RTX 4090 actually supports concurrent kernel execution. Checking the specs: it has compute capability 8.9, supports up to 128 concurrent kernels, and has 128 SMs, so 4 concurrent kernels would be well within limits.\n\nI'm confident this will work. Let me write up the implementation plan.\n\nActually, I should consider whether the `COPY_EVENT` mutex contention is a real problem. With 4 threads each calling to_host(), the mutex serializes these operations—each call takes roughly 12-17 microseconds when accounting for acquisition, the async copy, event recording, and release. Across 1246 total to_host() calls, that's around 19 milliseconds of D2H time, distributed across 4 threads doing roughly 312 calls each.\n\nBut there's a critical detail: if a thread launches a kernel and immediately calls to_host() before the kernel finishes, the cudaMemcpyAsync will block waiting for that kernel to complete—and while it's blocked, it holds the mutex, preventing other threads from doing their D2H transfers. However, those other threads can still launch their own kernels in parallel since kernel launches don't require the mutex. So the contention might not be as bad as it initially seems, since the threads can overlap kernel execution while waiting for D2H serialization to complete. the pipeline is:\n- Thread 1: launch → to_host (blocked waiting for kernel, holds mutex for 500μs) → process\n- Threads 2-4: launch → (try to_host, blocked by mutex) → wait → to_host → process\n- Meanwhile GPU: executes kernels from threads 1-4 concurrently\n\nThe mutex contention reduces the parallelism benefit. Instead of 4-way parallel D2H, we get serial D2H with the wait time of the l