nstraint_3 → (blocked) → ... \nThread 4: launch_constraint_4 → (blocked) → ...\n\nThe threads get serialized at the D2H step because of the mutex. This partially negates the multi-stream benefit.\n\nThe per-AIR time with 4 threads would be:\n- Prep: overlapped (parallel)\n- Kernel launch: overlapped (parallel, async)\n- Kernel execution: concurrent on GPU (multi-stream benefit)\n- D2H: SERIALIZED (mutex)\n- Postprocess: overlapped (parallel)\n\nThe real bottleneck is whichever takes longer—kernel execution or the serialized D2H transfers. Since each D2H holds the mutex for roughly kernel_time plus copy_time, and all 4 threads are queuing up behind that mutex, the total time for processing 4 AIRs ends up being similar to sequential processing. The multi-stream benefit only kicks in while thread 1 is blocked waiting for its kernel to finish, allowing other threads to launch their kernels in parallel. in to_host() (waiting for its kernel), threads 2-4 have already launched their kernels, which execute concurrently on the GPU. So when thread 2 finally gets the mutex, its kernel might already be done.\n\nLet me trace through the execution timeline: Thread 1 launches first and acquires the mutex, initiating a memcpy and blocking on its kernel's completion. Meanwhile, threads 2-4 queue up behind the mutex while their kernels run in parallel on the GPU. By the time thread 1's kernel finishes around 100μs and releases the mutex, threads 2-4's kernels have likely already completed, so they each acquire the mutex, perform their memcpy operations almost instantly, and release it in quick succession.\n\nThis batching effect means processing 4 AIRs takes roughly 130μs total instead of 400μs sequentially—about a 3x speedup. The bottleneck shifts from individual kernel execution to the maximum kernel time across the batch, since the GPU can overlap all four kernels while the CPU threads are serialized by the mutex.\n\nFor 623 AIRs split into batches of 4, that works out to around 3